{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>I will set over me a king like all the nations.</b> That the kingship shall be his and his seed's — not like the Judges, where the Judge ruled himself alone, but not his seed after him. Concerning the appointment of a Judge as king in this manner they were commanded at their entry into the Land, as it says: וְלֹא תִהְיֶה עֲדַת ה׳ כַּצֹּאן אֲשֶׁר אֵין לָהֶם רֹעֶה (Numbers 27:17). That he should be like the kings of the nations — holding the kingship for himself and his seed — was abhorrent to the Blessed El. But He commanded that when they stiffen their neck to set over themselves a king in this manner, they shall choose none but a worthy man whom HaShem would choose (Deuteronomy 17:15), and he shall not turn Israel aside from the faith, and he shall not be a foreigner (Deuteronomy 17:15), even if he be a worthy man, and even if he be a mighty warrior. And when they sinned in asking for a king who would rule — he and his seed — like all the nations (I Samuel 8:5), their punishment was through the misfortunes that befall the multitude on account of the king, as it says: וּזְעַקְתֶּם בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא מִלִּפְנֵי מַלְכְּכֶם אֲשֶׁר בְּחַרְתֶּם לָכֶם, וְלֹא יַעֲנֶה ה׳ אֶתְכֶם בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא (I Samuel 8:18), and as it says: אֶתֶּן לְךָ מֶלֶךְ בְּאַפִּי, וְאֶקַּח בְּעֶבְרָתִי (Hosea 13:11). And the permission regarding the appointment of the king was like the permission regarding יְפַת תֹּאַר (the beautiful woman), which hinted that the end result is that he will come to hate her and beget from her a wayward and rebellious son — as came to pass with David through the matter of Absalom."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:21:49.098660467Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}